This is Erdős Problem 749, a research-level open problem in additive combinatorics marked as `open` in the Formal Conjectures library.

The problem asks whether for every ε > 0, there exists a set A ⊆ ℕ such that:
1. The lower density of A + A is at least 1 − ε, and
2. The representation function 1_A ∗ 1_A(n) is bounded by a constant C(ε) for all n.

This sits in tension between two competing requirements: making A + A nearly all of ℕ (which typically requires A to be "large" or "spread out") while keeping the number of representations of each n uniformly bounded (which requires A to be "thin" or "Sidon-like"). Sidon sets achieve bounded representations but have A + A of density zero; dense sets achieve large A + A but have unbounded representations. Whether one can simultaneously achieve lower density of A + A arbitrarily close to 1 with uniformly bounded representations is an open question that, to my knowledge, has not been resolved in the literature.

